  "answer_text": "The famous \"Del Boy falling through the bar\" scene is from the episode titled **\"Yuppy Love\"** (Series 6, Episode 1), which first aired on the BBC on **8 January 1989**. \n\nIt is widely considered one of the greatest, most iconic comedy moments in British television history.\n\n---\n\n### **The Context**\nDel Boy (David Jason) has watched the movie *Wall Street* and decided to completely reinvent himself as a modern, upwardly-mobile \"yuppie\". He swaps his signature flat cap and sheepskin coat for a green trench coat, a striped shirt, red braces, a silver metal briefcase, and a Filofax. \n\nHoping to live the high life and meet sophisticated women, Del and Trigger (Roger Lloyd Pack) ditch their usual local pub, *The Nag\u2019s Head*, to hang out in a trendy, upscale wine bar. \n\n### **The Scene**\nDel spots two attractive women at the bar and decides to show Trigger how to \"charm\" them. Trying to act as suave and sophisticated as possible, Del leans in to Trigger and says: \n\n> **Del Boy:** *\"I think we're on a winner here, Trig. Play it nice and cool, son, nice and cool...\"*\n\nDel looks over at the women, gives them a smug smile, and prepares to lean his elbow casually back onto the bar flap. \n\nJust seconds earlier, however, the bartender (played by William Thomas) had lifted the hinged wooden bar flap to walk through, leaving a wide open gap. \n\nDel leans back\u2014expecting the bar to be there\u2014and **falls completely straight down through the open hatch**, disappearing instantly from the frame. \n\n### **The Reaction**\nWhat makes the scene comedy gold is Trigger's delayed reaction. Completely oblivious to what just happened, Trigger does a brilliant, slow-paced double-take. He looks around in utter bewilderment, wondering where on earth Del has gone. \n\nWhen Del finally climbs back up to his feet, dusting himself off, he immediately tries to save face:\n\n> **Del:** *\"Drink up, Trig, drink up, we're leaving.\"*\n> \n> **Trigger:** *\"Ain't you gonna try it for them birds?\"*\n> \n> **Del:** *\"Nah, nah, you're cramping my style, mate, you're cramping my style!\"*\n\n---\n\n### **Behind-the-Scenes Facts**\n* **Trigger wasn\u2019t supposed to be in the scene:** A rehearsal script revealed that Trigger was never originally meant to be part of this bar scene. However, Roger Lloyd Pack happened to show up on set that day in his blue suit. Writer John Sullivan decided to write him in at the last minute\u2014a decision that made the scene legendary.\n* **Filmed in only one take:** Because of how perfectly David Jason executed the physical comedy, the production crew only had to film the fall once. \n* **The \"rigid\" fall:** David Jason began his career in physical theatre and slapstick. If you watch the scene closely, he falls completely rigid like a falling plank of wood without his legs buckling. Because he didn't try to instinctively brace himself or break his fall, it looked incredibly sudden, natural, and hilarious. \n* **Based on a true story:** Writer John Sullivan got the idea from a real-life incident where he saw a man in a pub go to lean on a bar flap, not realizing it had been raised. In real life, the man managed to catch himself, but Sullivan knew it would be much funnier if Del Boy fell all the way through.",
